𝟭. 𝗘𝘅𝗲𝗰𝘂𝘁𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗔𝘀𝘀𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁

A short, intense bombing campaign under the priority lists currently under discussion does not advance the stated US national interest of a nuclear-free Iran and will cause more harm to regional partners and the global economy than it removes. The circulating target set centers on Pickaxe Mountain, residual nuclear-related sites, selected energy and transport connectors, and limited military positions. It does not place Iran’s reconstituted retaliatory arsenal—residual ballistic stocks, recoverable launchers, underground assembly capacity, and coastal anti-ship networks—first after target-area air dominance or under ongoing suppression. Neither partner has assigned that mission. The United States can pulse forces; it cannot sustain high-tempo operations. Past and present logistical and basing constraints allowed reconstitution. The midterm result adds a domestic political limit and a negotiation asymmetry. Short strikes that leave the lethal arsenal intact do not force earlier nuclear commitments, do not reduce Iran’s ability to deny commercial passage or strike partner infrastructure, and transmit further costs into fuel prices, freight, and the US economy through Iranian retaliation.

𝟮. 𝗖𝘂𝗿𝗿𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗘𝗻𝘃𝗶𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗠𝗶𝗱𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗺 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝘂𝗹𝘁

The 3 November midterm results, in which Democrats took both chambers, confirm that the war’s economic transmission registered with voters and produced a measurable domestic political cost. Exit and pre-election polling treated living costs and the economy as the leading factor (Reuters: nearly nine in ten called it major or crucial). The Iran War was separately rated high as a “crucial or major” factor (74% in cited exit polling) and as “extremely important” by 47% in Gallup. The measurable channel was fuel: gasoline roughly 40–45% above pre-war levels in late reporting, diesel higher. A Chicago Council survey found 86% of adults said the war had been bad for the cost of living. Iranian residual ballistic stocks, recoverable launchers, coastal anti-ship networks, and Houthi employment generated the repeated low-cost effects that produced those price increases. US and Israeli strikes that left those systems off the priority list after air dominance did not remove the means of generating the tax.

The result is both a propaganda win and a strategic gain for Iran. Iranian messaging has long framed the war as a contest of endurance in which US domestic politics, not battlefield attrition, would set the limit on continuation. A Democratic Congress is not hands-off. It can investigate, hold hearings, condition or delay supplemental funding, and publicly frame further strikes as an extension of an unpopular policy. These tools do not eliminate the President’s ability to order a short pulse, but they raise the political and resource cost of sustaining even that pulse and of accepting the next round of Iranian and Houthi retaliation against partner infrastructure. Iran therefore enters any negotiation with an improved position. A formal agreement would face Senate advice and consent if cast as a treaty, or sustained congressional scrutiny and potential funding limits if handled as an executive understanding.

Indirect talks mediated by Qatar continue. Iran’s seven-condition plan, now condensed to a two-day implementation timeline for reopening Hormuz, remains the stated Iranian position. Those conditions include ending the US naval blockade, lifting oil sanctions, releasing frozen assets, ending hostilities on all fronts including Lebanon, accepting an Iran-Oman transit route under Iranian supervision, and reducing US forces. The US has sought nuclear commitments earlier in the sequence, including specific steps on highly enriched uranium. Iranian officials describe the US response as “not convincing.” No agreement has been reached.

𝟯. 𝗨𝗦 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗜𝘀𝗿𝗮𝗲𝗹𝗶 𝗧𝗮𝗿𝗴𝗲𝘁 𝗦𝗲𝘁𝘀 𝘂𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗿 𝗗𝗶𝘀𝗰𝘂𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻

Open reporting on potential post-midterm strikes centers on a narrow set. Pickaxe Mountain (the deeply buried complex near Natanz) has been singled out by the President as a short-duration option that could be executed in hours. Residual nuclear-related sites (Fordow, Arak, and related support) remain listed despite prior strikes. Energy and transport nodes appear repeatedly: electricity and gas facilities, selected refineries and terminals, railways, bridges, and certain coastal military positions. Limited military targets of the type struck at Qeshm (exposed surface-to-air positions) continue to be framed as defensive or preparatory. Israeli priorities have emphasized launcher attrition, nuclear-related sites, and selected production nodes, yet residual assessments still show substantial surviving ballistic stocks and recoverable launchers. These are the categories that have circulated. Energy and transport connectors can impose friction on Iranian capacity and compound the effects of the blockade. They are not the underground storage, recoverable mobile launchers, or coastal anti-ship complexes that generate the salvos Iran has already demonstrated.

Target-area air dominance must be established before strike aircraft can complete their missions. That dominance is produced by suppression of enemy air defenses (SEAD), destruction of enemy air defenses (DEAD), and related measures that clear the immediate operating environment. Once established, it allows the follow-on strikes to proceed. It does not itself select or destroy the retaliatory arsenal. The arsenal remains a separate targeting requirement.

𝟰. 𝗜𝗿𝗮𝗻𝗶𝗮𝗻 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗛𝗼𝘂𝘁𝗵𝗶 𝗥𝗲𝘁𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗿𝗴𝗲𝘁𝘀

Iranian statements and prior practice point to a different set. Mojtaba Khamenei directed an update of the target bank for a response without geographical limits covering US and Israeli physical and non-physical assets. Iranian media and commanders have referenced US support bases and infrastructure in Europe, NATO facilities, and Diego Garcia. Within the region the named categories are consistent: active bases that still generate sorties, energy infrastructure, oil and gas facilities, airports, bridges, railways, petrochemical plants, power plants, desalination plants, undersea cables, data centers, AI facilities, and selected civil nodes. Senior figures have stated that the economic architecture enabling pressure on Iran will not remain immune. Houthi employment extends the same logic to shipping in the Bab el-Mandeb approaches, Saudi energy and airport targets, and related logistics. Past strikes followed the pattern of early-warning systems and supporting base infrastructure first, then energy and civil nodes that transmit costs into markets and alliance politics. Already-degraded bases offer lower marginal return; active bases, energy systems, and civil infrastructure remain higher-value for raising the price of continuation.

Iranian leaders have also stated that retaliatory strikes could extend outside the region. The same “without geographical limits” directive covers US and Israeli assets beyond the Middle East, including support bases and infrastructure in Europe. Non-physical assets explicitly include cyberspace. Current Iranian cyber activity centers on established IRGC-linked groups that have conducted espionage, credential theft, and disruptive operations against energy, government, and critical-infrastructure targets in the Gulf, Europe, and the United States. Documented patterns include wiper malware against industrial control systems, phishing and supply-chain intrusion against energy and logistics firms, and influence operations. Potential retaliatory cyber activity would likely expand these lines: targeting energy-sector SCADA and pipeline control systems, financial clearing and payment networks, undersea cable landing stations or associated data centers, and logistics software used by US and partner forces. The effect would be to impose friction on the same economic and transit systems already named as kinetic targets, without requiring physical salvos. These are claims of capability and intent until executed; the existing groups and prior operations against energy and government networks provide the baseline.

𝟱. 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗔𝗿𝘀𝗲𝗻𝗮𝗹 𝗚𝗮𝗽

The circulating US and Israeli lists do not prioritize the assets Iran will use for those retaliatory strikes. Recent Israeli assessments place residual ballistic missiles capable of reaching Israel at approximately 1,700, with additional shorter-range stocks for Gulf bases. Operational launchers are assessed near 400—close to pre-war levels—after recovery from sealed tunnels. Underground assembly from pre-stockpiled engines, guidance sections, and airframes continues even where solid-propellant production facilities remain damaged. Coastal anti-ship networks on Qeshm and the mainland were not the target of the recent SAM-focused strikes; IRGC Navy tunnels and storage remain. These are the systems that enable multi-day barrages, shipping pressure, and base disruption.

Destruction or suppression of this retaliatory arsenal must be part of the initial strikes once target-area air dominance is established, and it must be ongoing. If the residual stocks, recoverable launchers, underground assembly capacity, and coastal anti-ship networks are not on the initial target list and continuous pressure is not applied to them, the retaliatory strikes that follow will be severe and destructive. Temporary cave-ins or single-pass destruction of exposed units do not remove the inventory or the recoverable systems. Because the arsenal is not on the priority list, the strikes that do occur leave the means of retaliation intact. Neither partner currently has sustained suppression of the reconstituted retaliatory arsenal as a priority mission after air dominance. No public allocation assigns either partner the sustained post-strike suppression task. Past joint efforts produced temporary cave-ins that Iranian crews reopened; they did not eliminate the inventory or the recoverable units.

𝟲. 𝗙𝗮𝗶𝗹𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝘁𝗼 𝗠𝗮𝗶𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗶𝗻 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗶𝗻𝘂𝗼𝘂𝘀 𝗦𝘂𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻

Episodic strikes that occurred were generally concentrated in a nighttime window (roughly midnight to 4 a.m.). That pattern created a schedule Iran could operate around. Crews adjusted movement, excavation, and assembly outside the known period. Launchers could be staged or recovered in daylight and early evening; tunnel work and component movement avoided the recurring window. The result was not continuous pressure that forced constant dispersal. It was predictable risk reduction that left the rest of the day for reconstitution. Assessments of restored launcher numbers and ongoing underground assembly are consistent with that adaptation. Around-the-clock response to presenting threats would have removed reliable safe periods, complicated crew cycles, and raised the cumulative cost of keeping storage and launch capacity accessible. The actual tempo conserved US sorties and tanker demand under standoff basing and magazine constraints, but it also supplied the operational predictability that allowed recovery. Infrastructure connector strikes can still impose longer-term friction. They do not substitute for continuous pressure on the arsenal itself.

𝟳. 𝗥𝗲𝗴𝗶𝗺𝗲 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗿𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗲𝗿, 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗶𝗹𝗶𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗲, 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗦𝘁𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗴𝗶𝗰 𝗥𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝗮𝗹𝗲

Iran is an Islamic revolutionary state. Its leadership and core forces operate under a framework that treats martyrdom as an acceptable cost and does not face the constraints of election cycles, polling, or independent media. Regime ground forces remain unscathed and fully operational. Missile and drone capacity has been reconstituted to the degree shown in residual stock and launcher assessments. These factors reduce the political and domestic friction that would constrain a conventional state under sustained pressure. The willingness to absorb costs and continue operations is consistent with the pattern of recovery after prior strike phases and the explicit statements of expanded targeting without geographical limits.

Nearly fifty years of sanctions, isolation, and repeated conflict have hardened the regime and the people further. The 1979 revolution, the Iran-Iraq War, successive sanction regimes, and the current campaign have produced institutions and a population that treat external pressure as a permanent condition rather than a temporary crisis. Parallel import networks, domestic production of selected military components, and the IRGC’s economic role developed under these constraints. Public framing of endurance as resistance has been sustained across generations under the same conditions. The result is a higher tolerance for economic pain and a demonstrated capacity to reconstitute military systems from stockpiled or improvised inputs. This history raises the threshold at which pressure produces political change inside the regime or broad popular rejection of continued confrontation.

Iran’s rationale for escalation rests on relative costs and the survival of its retaliatory means. The residual ballistic inventory, recoverable launchers, underground assembly capacity, and coastal anti-ship networks allow continued generation of attacks against active bases, energy infrastructure, and transit routes. Each US or Israeli strike that does not remove those means still triggers a response that imposes defensive expenditure, base disruption, and market costs on the United States and partners. Because Iran is not constrained by electoral cycles or independent media, and because its leadership framework accepts high costs including martyrdom, the political price of continuation falls more heavily on the side that must sustain expensive operations and protect commercial recovery. Expanded targeting without geographical limits, including cyber operations against energy and transit systems, is the logical extension: raise the economic and alliance costs until the cumulative war tax exceeds the value of further strikes. Houthi coordination multiplies the effect across separated fronts without requiring Iran to expend its full inventory in a single exchange. The objective is not conventional battlefield victory. It is to keep the denial mechanism intact and the cost distribution unfavorable until the opposing side adjusts its objectives or tempo.

𝟴. 𝗖𝗮𝗽𝗮𝗰𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗟𝗶𝗺𝗶𝘁𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗧𝗮𝗿𝗴𝗲𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗖𝗵𝗼𝗶𝗰𝗲

Two capacity constraints apply and must be kept separate.

The past and present constraint is logistical and basing. Degraded regional basing after Iranian strikes on early-warning and support infrastructure, inventory shortfalls across munitions, aircraft, ships, and carrier air wings, and the tanker demand created by standoff distances have already restricted operations to episodic pulses. That limit is why continuous suppression did not occur and why Iran was able to excavate tunnels, recover launchers toward pre-war numbers, and continue underground assembly. The result is a largely reconstituted retaliatory arsenal.

The future constraint is different. The United States does not have the capacity to execute an extended high-tempo air campaign. It can pulse forces for a short, intense effort. It cannot sustain that tempo long enough to prevent regeneration over time. Because the available pulse is finite, the targeting choice becomes decisive.

Target-area air dominance must be established first by SEAD, DEAD, and related measures so that strike aircraft can complete their missions. Once that dominance is in place, destruction or suppression of the Iranian retaliatory arsenal must be part of the initial strikes and must be ongoing. Residual ballistic stocks, recoverable launchers, underground assembly capacity, and coastal anti-ship networks have to be on the initial target list. If they are not, and if continuous pressure is not applied to them, the retaliatory strikes that follow will be severe and destructive against regional partner infrastructure and the people who live and work around it. The effects transmit directly into the global economy: elevated fuel prices, higher war-risk and freight costs, disrupted commercial passage through Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb approaches, and cumulative losses for importing economies. Cyber operations against the same sectors add non-kinetic friction. This is damage to partner systems and civilian lives that then becomes a global war tax.

𝟵. 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗹𝘂𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻

The stated US national interest is a nuclear-free Iran. A short intense bombing campaign under the priority lists currently under discussion does not advance that interest and will cause more harm to regional partners and the global economy than it removes. The circulating target set leaves Iran’s reconstituted retaliatory arsenal—residual ballistic stocks, recoverable launchers, underground assembly capacity, and coastal anti-ship networks—off the initial list after target-area air dominance and under no assigned ongoing suppression. Neither partner has made sustained pressure on those systems a priority mission. The United States can pulse forces; it cannot sustain high-tempo operations. Past and present logistical and basing constraints allowed reconstitution. The midterm result adds a domestic political limit and a negotiation asymmetry.

Short strikes that leave the lethal arsenal intact do not force earlier nuclear commitments, do not reduce Iran’s ability to deny commercial passage or strike partner infrastructure, and transmit further costs into fuel prices, freight, and the US economy through Iranian retaliation. They are more likely to trigger the retaliatory strikes already analyzed—against active bases, partner energy and transit nodes, and the people who live and work around them—than to secure an agreement on American terms. The interest, as currently pursued, accepts the continuation of a lethal conventional arsenal capable of threatening neighbors and imposing costs. Without placing that arsenal on the initial target list and under continuous pressure after air dominance, further short strikes do not align means with the objective of reducing Iran’s coercive capacity. The United States continues to pay the higher relative cost while leaving Iran the means to impose the war tax that has already produced a congressional shift and an improved Iranian negotiating position.

